Chapter 25 — Part 1
Curtis left the city after the café. The train took him out past the suburbs until the buildings thinned and the tree lines took over. He walked the rest of the way on foot, red cap low, jacket open, boots finding the quieter paths without effort.
The Japanese forests were green and orderly compared to what he knew. Birds. Clean trails. Too peaceful. It felt like a park that had forgotten how to be dangerous. He kept moving, looking for something that sat heavier.
He found it at the edge of Aokigahara.
The trees changed. Denser. Quieter. The light dropped even though the sun was still up. No birds. The air felt thick, like the woods back home when something old was watching. Signs in Japanese warned people to stay on the paths. He didn’t. He stepped off the trail and into the deeper dark, the low, steady pull in his chest finally stirring the way it used to in the Appalachian night.
For the first time since he’d landed in Japan, the forest felt close to familiar. He kept walking.
The deeper he went, the less the forest pretended to be ordinary. Moss swallowed sound. Roots twisted over old stone. Every few dozen meters a faded ribbon or a discarded shoe marked where someone had left the path and not come back. Curtis moved through it without slowing, boots quiet, sunglasses now hooked on his shirt. Heterochromia tracked the dark between the trees.
He stopped when the air changed again. Colder. The low, steady pull in his chest sharpened—the same warning it used to give in the mountains when a cryptid was close. Something was here. Not human. Not recent.
A shape shifted between two trunks ahead. Tall. Too many joints. It didn’t step into the light. It just watched. Curtis watched back. No fear. Just recognition.
“You’re a long way from Appalachia,” he said, voice low. “Or I’m a long way from home.”
The shape didn’t answer. It didn’t attack. It simply existed in the dark the way the old things always had. Curtis nodded once, like they’d agreed on something, and kept walking. The forest stayed silent around him. For the first time in weeks the quiet felt correct.
Curtis stopped again. He faced the dark between the trunks fully.
“I am here to train. I recognized your presence and you picked up on mine.”
The shape shifted. Joints clicked softly. When it spoke, the voice was dry, layered, like wind through dead leaves.
“Not… human… are you?”
Curtis smirked. Heterochromia caught what little light filtered through the canopy. “Who knows?”
The thing didn’t advance. It didn’t retreat. The forest stayed silent around them. Curtis rolled his neck, jacket shifting, and set his feet on the uneven root.
“You can watch. Or you can test me. Either way, I’m not leaving until I’ve worked.”
The low, steady pull in his chest had settled into the old, familiar readiness. The shape in the dark remained where it was. For the moment, that was answer enough.
Dusk had turned the edge of Aokigahara the color of a bruise. Curtis stepped onto the outer path with his jacket torn at the sleeve and dirt ground into the knees of his pants. He looked untouched otherwise. Calm. He turned back toward the tree line, raised one hand, and waved at nothing.
“Later.”
The dark between the trunks did not move. No wind. No birds. Just the sense that something tall had shifted one step deeper so it would not be seen.
A couple on the trail stopped dead. The woman made a small, choked sound and grabbed her partner’s coat hard enough to wrinkle it. Both of them had gone bloodless. People who entered Aokigahara at this hour did not come back out waving. They did not smile faintly at empty air like they were leaving a neighbor’s porch. The man’s phone was already in his hand, but he couldn’t make his thumb work.
Curtis glanced at them. Heterochromia caught the last light under the cap. He gave a short, polite nod—as if their fear was noted and irrelevant—then kept walking toward the road. His boots were the only sound. The couple stayed frozen until he was fifty meters gone. Only then did the woman whisper, voice shaking:
“He waved at it.”
Behind them the forest remained perfectly still. Whatever had watched Curtis train did not follow. It simply waited, and the low, steady pull in his chest had already gone quiet by the time the streetlights began.
Curtis made it back to the suite with the streetlights already on. He stripped the casual clothes, rinsed the dirt off, and changed into the work uniform—black button-down, red tie, white suspenders, white pants. The cut on his lip was fresh, split from whatever he’d traded strikes with in the forest. He checked it in the small mirror, frowned, and muttered.
“Shit. I hope it doesn’t make the customers walk away.”
He locked the door and stepped into the stairwell. Hina was already coming down from her floor, dressed for Lumen, bag on her shoulder. She stopped when she saw him, eyes catching the cut, then the uniform.
“Oh. Hey, Hina.” Curtis adjusted a suspender. “Wanna walk to work together?”
She smiled, small and warm, the flush already starting. “Yeah. I’d like that.”
They fell in side by side on the sidewalk. The city moved around them. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the cut on his lip and the unfinished day sat between them, and the club waited a few stations ahead.
They walked under the station lights. Hina glanced at the split on his lip for the third time, then finally asked.
“What happened to your mouth?”
Curtis touched it with his thumb, checking if it had opened again. “Training. Went out to the forest. Something out there wanted to see if I was worth the trouble.” He shrugged. “It was. Mostly.”
Hina’s brow pulled. “Aokigahara? People don’t just train there.”
“I do.” His tone stayed even. “Felt more like home than the city parks. Cut’s nothing. Had worse from less.”
She didn’t push. She just stayed close enough that their arms brushed every few steps, the worry sitting quiet under the usual warmth. “Janice is gonna notice.”
“I’ll tell her I walked into a door.” The corner of his mouth twitched, careful of the cut. “Or that a dullahan got handsy. Your pick.”
Hina laughed despite herself, soft. They kept walking. The club wasn’t far now, and the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the city lights grew brighter ahead of them.
Janice spotted the cut the second Curtis and Hina came through the side door. She was behind the bar restocking, Tatsuya beside her with the tablet. Her eyes narrowed.
“What the hell happened to your face?”
Curtis touched the split again. “Training. Forest. Nothing serious.”
“Nothing serious looks like you got punched by a tree.” She set the bottle down and came around the bar, tilting his chin with two fingers to check it under the light. The touch was clinical. “Customers notice faces. Especially on you. If it opens while you’re on the floor, you’re explaining blood to someone who paid for a smile.”
Tatsuya glanced over, calm. “Ice in the back. Ten minutes. It’ll settle.”
Janice released his chin and pointed toward the staff room. “Ice. Then you’re on. And next time you decide to wrestle the woods, do it on a day you’re not scheduled. I already kept you off yesterday.”
“Yes, ma’am.” Curtis’s tone was even. He headed for the ice. Hina slipped past to clock in, shooting him a small look. Janice watched him go, arms crossed, the irritation real but the worry sitting right under it. She turned back to the bar once he was out of sight.
“Kids,” she muttered. Tatsuya just hummed and kept working. The cut would be fine. The fact that he’d walked in with it like it was normal was the part she didn’t like.
The cut did the opposite of what Curtis expected.
Regulars noticed it the moment he stepped onto the floor. Instead of pulling back, they leaned in. A woman at his first table reached up without thinking, fingers hovering near the split. “Are you okay? That looks fresh.” Another guest on the next round did the same, concerned and already flushed. Free Love made the worry warmer. Hands kept drifting toward his face like checking the injury was an excuse they didn’t have to name.
Janice caught the third one. She appeared at the table’s edge, smile professional and sharp. “No touching the hosts. House rule. He’s fine.”
Tatsuya handled the next. He stepped in smooth, redirected the hand with a polite wrist touch of his own, and repeated the line. “Faces stay hands-off. He’s working.”
They had to do it twice more before the hour was up. Curtis sat through it, expression even, letting them enforce the boundary so he didn’t have to. The cut stayed closed. The regulars stayed seated. By the time his first break hit, Janice met him at the staff door with an ice pack and a look.
“Next time you show up marked, I’m putting you on bar support until it fades. They’re treating it like an invitation.”
“Noted.” He took the ice. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the floor kept moving and the touching rule got enforced one gentle redirect at a time.
By the end of the night the cut had paid for itself. Pity tips stacked higher than usual—extra bills slipped across the table with soft “feel better” comments and lingering looks. Curtis counted them in the staff room without comment and set the bundle aside for the lockbox.
Janice leaned in the doorway, arms crossed, while the floor shut down. “Heard you’re in the Sports Festival. U.A. thing. Two weeks.”
“Didn’t want to be,” Curtis said. He peeled the tie loose. “Got convinced. Classmate met a condition I set. So I’m in.”
She studied the split on his lip. “And the training that did that?”
“Extreme body conditioning. Wrestling with other species.” His tone stayed even. “Forest. Things that don’t pull punches. Keeps the edge where it needs to be.”
Janice’s brow rose. “So once the Festival’s over, you won’t need to train like that anymore.”
Curtis tilted his head. Heterochromia caught the staff-room light. “I train all the time. Not just for a Festival.”
She held the look for a second, then huffed. “Of course you do. Ice that lip again before you leave. And if you show up tomorrow with a matching one on the other side, you’re on glass duty.”
“Understood.” He grabbed his jacket. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the club locked up around them and the next two weeks of unwanted stages and ordinary shifts sat waiting outside.
Janice didn’t leave the doorway. She watched him pull the jacket on. “What’s the style, then? You move like you’ve done more than school drills.”
Curtis paused with one arm in the sleeve. “No formal style. Grew up handling things that didn’t follow rules. So I don’t either.” He settled the jacket. “Close range. Break what’s useful. Don’t give them time to use whatever makes them dangerous. If it bleeds or breaks, it can be stopped. Rest is just noise.”
She was quiet for a second. The club lights had dimmed to closing levels. “That forest cut. Something actually tried to kill you today?”
“Tested,” he said. “There’s a difference. Most nights.”
Janice exhaled through her nose. “And you’re doing this between shifts and a hero school that just put you on national television.” She shook her head once. “You’re either going to be very expensive to insure or very hard to kill. Try not to make me find out which before the Festival.”
“I’ll aim for the second.” The corner of his mouth twitched, careful of the split. “See you tomorrow.”
She stepped aside. He headed for the side door. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the city night waited outside and Janice watched him go, already knowing the ice pack wouldn’t be the last one she handed him.
